{"id":"812953d1-6869-4f41-9be4-dd48e3927ee6","slug":"india-airport-operators-own-airlines-aviation-duopoly-policy","title":"India Weighs Letting Airport Operators Launch Airlines to Break Aviation Duopoly","excerpt":"With IndiGo and Air India controlling over 90% of domestic flights, the Centre is exploring whether airport operators should be permitted to own carriers — a move that could reshape Indian aviation.","body":"<!-- data-card -->\n<div class=\"vdc-takeaways\"><div class=\"vdc-takeaways-title\">Key Takeaways</div><ul><li>The Indian government is considering allowing airport operators to own airlines to increase competition.</li><li>IndiGo and Air India dominate over 90% of India's domestic aviation market, limiting passenger choices.</li><li>Passenger growth in India's aviation sector has slowed to just 1.44% in the first half of 2026.</li><li>The Adani Group plans to invest $11.1 billion in airport infrastructure by 2030 to enhance capacity.</li></ul></div>\n\n<!-- data-card -->\n<div class=\"vdc\"><div class=\"vdc-glow\"></div><div class=\"vdc-title\">Aviation Market Dominance</div><div class=\"vdc-hero\"><div class=\"vdc-hero-num\">90%</div><div class=\"vdc-hero-label\">Market Share of IndiGo and Air India Combined</div></div><div class=\"vdc-grid\"><div class=\"vdc-stat\"><div class=\"vdc-stat-val\">64.3%</div><div class=\"vdc-stat-lbl\">IndiGo Market Share</div></div><div class=\"vdc-stat\"><div class=\"vdc-stat-val\">25.7%</div><div class=\"vdc-stat-lbl\">Air India Market Share</div></div><div class=\"vdc-stat\"><div class=\"vdc-stat-val\">514</div><div class=\"vdc-stat-lbl\">Routes Operated Solely by IndiGo</div></div><div class=\"vdc-stat\"><div class=\"vdc-stat-val\">1.44%</div><div class=\"vdc-stat-lbl\">Passenger Growth Rate (H1 2026)</div></div></div><div class=\"vdc-footer\">Data from the Ministry of Civil Aviation</div></div>\n\n> **\"Go First and Jet Airways have shut down.\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:8px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px\">Jet Airways.\"**\n\n<p>The Indian government is considering a potentially transformative change to the country's aviation landscape: allowing airport operators to own airlines, a move aimed at breaking the tightening grip of a two-player market that has left passengers with dwindling choices and rising fares.</p><h2>A Market Cornered by Two</h2><p>India's domestic aviation sector has consolidated dramatically. IndiGo commands a 64.3% market share, while the Tata Group-controlled Air India Group holds 25.7%, according to data from the Ministry of Civil Aviation. Together, the two groups account for more than 90% of domestic passengers — a concentration that has intensified as competitors have either collapsed or been absorbed.</p>\n<p>Go First and Jet Airways have shut down. SpiceJet clings to a shrinking footprint. Regional carriers like Star Air and Fly91 serve niche routes but lack the scale to challenge the incumbents. Of the roughly 1,131 domestic routes, IndiGo alone is the sole operator on 514 — more than 57% of its network.</p><p>Passenger growth, meanwhile, has stalled. Domestic carriers flew 864 lakh passengers in the first half of 2026, up just 1.44% from a year earlier, with traffic actually declining month-over-month in June.</p>\n\n<!-- data-card -->\n<div class=\"vdc\"><div class=\"vdc-glow\"></div><div class=\"vdc-title\">Adani Group's Ambitious Plans</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Adani Group operates seven airports, including Mumbai.</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Plans to invest $11.1 billion by 2030 in airport infrastructure.</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Aims to enhance capacity and expand international flying rights.</div></div>\n\n<figure style=\"margin:28px 0;text-align:center\"><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/VT-JEK_Boeing_777_Jet_Airways_%2814764668176%29.jpg\" alt=\"Jet Airways. Go First and Jet Airways have shut down.\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:8px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:0.85rem;color:#666;margin-top:8px\">Jet Airways. Go First and Jet Airways have shut down.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The Airport Operator Angle</h2><p>The policy under consideration would permit companies that build and manage airports — entities like the Adani Group, GMR and others — to also hold stakes in or operate airlines. The thinking, according to the Storyboard18 report, is that airport operators have the infrastructure, capital and commercial incentive to bring more carriers into service.</p><blockquote class=\"pull-quote\"><p>\"This will be a criminal waste of assets being built by airports and penalising the Indian customers who will have to pay higher prices due to lack of flights.\"</p><cite>— Adani Group official, on the need for expanded airline capacity</cite></blockquote><p>The Adani Group operates seven airports, including Mumbai — one of the country's most capacity-constrained hubs — and recently launched commercial operations at the new Navi Mumbai airport. The group has outlined plans to invest $11.1 billion by 2030 in terminals, runways and passenger facilities, and has separately argued for expanded international flying rights to drive traffic through its hubs.</p><h2>Regulatory and Competition Questions</h2><p>The proposal is not without complications. Critics argue that allowing airport operators to own airlines could create conflicts of interest, where a company managing terminal infrastructure might favour its own carrier over competitors in slot allocation, ground handling or service provision.</p><p>India's aviation sector already faces regulatory tension. The Competition Commission of India opened an antitrust probe into IndiGo after finding prima facie evidence of abuse of dominance, triggered by the mass cancellation of thousands of flights in late 2025 that left passengers stranded and sent fares soaring.</p><p>The Directorate General of Civil Aviation subsequently ordered IndiGo to cut flights, and the government doubled the mandated reduction to 10% — an unprecedented intervention that underscored just how dependent the system had become on a single carrier.</p><h2>Why It Matters for Travelers</h2><p>For passengers, particularly those on international routes connecting diaspora hubs in the United States, United Kingdom, Canada and the Gulf states, the duopoly has translated into fewer scheduling options and higher baseline fares. Air India has invested in fleet upgrades and new interiors but faces a long road to matching IndiGo's operational efficiency.</p><p>New entrants face formidable barriers. The gap between receiving a no-objection certificate and actually operating an airline remains vast — Air Kerala, for instance, received its clearance in 2024 but has yet to induct a single aircraft. Without access to airport slots, competitive fuel pricing and regulatory coordination, startups struggle to gain traction.</p><p>Whether airport operators can bridge that gap remains an open question. But for a sector where one airline's scheduling problems can cascade into a national travel crisis, the government's willingness to explore unconventional solutions signals the severity of the problem.</p>\n\n<!-- data-card -->\n<div class=\"vdc\"><div class=\"vdc-glow\"></div><div class=\"vdc-title\">Regulatory Challenges Ahead</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Competition Commission of India probes IndiGo for potential dominance abuse.</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Mass flight cancellations in late 2025 triggered regulatory scrutiny.</div><div class=\"vdc-bullet\"><span class=\"vdc-bullet-arrow\">›</span>Government mandated a 10% flight reduction for IndiGo.</div></div>","category":"news","hero_image_url":"https://lboecaekpynbpyijrbfz.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/article-images/tweet-hero-india-airport-operators-own-airlines-aviation-duopoly-policy-341d7dc9.jpg","image_caption":"Image: @thenewsminute via X","image_credit":"Pexels / Jaison Jacob Samuel","gallery_images":null,"published_at":"2026-07-22T05:43:28.902654+00:00","created_at":"2026-07-22T05:43:28.902654+00:00","status":"published","sources":[{"label":"https://www.outlookbusiness.com/news/indigo-air-india-group-control-over-90-of-domestic-market-as-passenger-growth-slows-in-h1"},{"label":"https://www.policycircle.org/industry/duopoly-in-aviation-sector-cci/"}],"nri_angle":"Indian travelers abroad have felt the duopoly's effects through higher fares and reduced route options, particularly on international sectors connecting diaspora hubs.","article_type":"breaking","tags":["Indian aviation","airport operators","IndiGo","Air India","duopoly","aviation policy","Adani Group"],"author":"Diaspora Desk","featured_score":12.840313413425925,"is_pinned_featured":false,"pinned_until":null,"event_at":null,"focal_x":0.5,"focal_y":0.5,"social_embeds":[]}